---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { effects } from '../../content/effects';
import { codeTheme } from '../../content/examples';
import Documentation from '../../layouts/Documentation.astro';
---
<Documentation>
  <p>Import attachment functions from <code>@domfx/browser</code> and optional timeline bindings from <code>@domfx/gsap</code>. Imports are SSR-safe. Call attachment functions only in the browser, after the target and host are mounted.</p>

  <h2 id="available-effects">Available effects</h2>
  <p>Each function takes <code>(target, options)</code> and immediately returns an <code>EffectHandle</code>. All {effects.length} effects share the options, controls and lifecycle below. Choose an appearance in the <a href="/effects/">effects gallery</a>.</p>
  <ul aria-label="Attachment functions">
    {effects.map((effect) => <li>{effect.name}: <code>{effect.exportName}</code></li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="create-an-attachment">Create an attachment</h2>
  <p>The browser package exports <code>AttachmentOptions</code> for the shared creation options. <code>FireOptions</code> extends it with <code>flameHeight</code>; the other effect option types use the shared contract.</p>
  <div class="docs-code-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Shared attachment options example"><Code code={`import { attachFrost } from '@domfx/browser';
import type { AttachmentOptions } from '@domfx/browser';

const options: AttachmentOptions = {
  host,
  clock: 'manual',
  motion: 'respect',
  seed: 314,
  time: 0,
  intensity: 0.8,
};
const effect = attachFrost(target, options);
// Register component teardown before waiting for initialization.
window.addEventListener('pagehide', () => effect.dispose(), { once: true });
await effect.ready;
if (effect.lifecycle === 'active') {
  effect.time = 2;
}`} lang="ts" theme={codeTheme} /></div>
  <ul>
    <li><code>host</code> (required): a connected, positioned HTML element containing the target as a strict descendant in the same document.</li>
    <li><code>clock</code>: <code>'manual'</code> by default. Use <code>'ambient'</code> to advance time automatically.</li>
    <li><code>motion</code>: <code>'respect'</code> by default, suppressing output under reduced motion. <code>'static'</code> paints a still decoration at time zero.</li>
    <li><code>seed</code>: defaults to 0. Must be finite and representable as float32. Fixed time and seed reproduce the effect on the same captured target.</li>
    <li><code>time</code>: absolute seconds, including negative values. Defaults to 0. Must be finite and representable as float32.</li>
    <li><code>intensity</code>: defaults to 1. Must be finite and representable as float32; accepted values clamp to 0–1. It controls each effect's appearance, rather than a shared physical height or opacity.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Creation options are fixed for the attachment's lifetime. Only <code>time</code> and <code>intensity</code> are writable on the handle. To change another option or the effect, dispose the attachment and create a replacement.</p>
  <p>Invalid options, host relationships and overlapping target ownership throw synchronously. A target, its ancestor and its descendants cannot have competing live attachments. Dispose the owner before attaching a replacement.</p>

  <h3 id="fire-only-options">Fire-only options</h3>
  <p><code>attachFire</code> also accepts <code>flameHeight</code>: 8–64 target-local CSS pixels, default 24. It sets the flame scale at creation. It is not a shared option and is not writable on the handle. The <a href="/docs/standalone#attach-fire">Standalone playground</a> recreates its Fire attachment when you change this value.</p>

  <h2 id="read-state">Read state</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ready: Promise&lt;void&gt;</code> settles after initial setup or cancelled-initialization cleanup. Environmental unavailability does not reject it; inspect lifecycle after awaiting it.</li>
    <li><code>lifecycle</code>: <code>'active'</code>, <code>'suspended'</code>, <code>'unavailable'</code> or <code>'disposed'</code>.</li>
    <li><code>raster</code>: <code>'fresh'</code>, <code>'refreshing'</code> or <code>'stale'</code>.</li>
    <li><code>reason</code>: a typed <code>EffectReason</code>, or <code>undefined</code>.</li>
    <li><code>reasonDetail</code>: diagnostic text, or <code>undefined</code>. Use <code>reason</code> for program logic; wording can change.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Playback, lifecycle and raster freshness are independent. A paused attachment can have a fresh raster and visible output. Readiness does not guarantee visible output or completed GPU execution.</p>

  <h2 id="control-playback">Control playback</h2>
  <p>Write <code>effect.time</code> and <code>effect.intensity</code> using the same value constraints as the creation options. Values are immediately readable; rendering batches on the next frame without recapturing the target. <code>seed</code> and <code>clock</code> are read-only.</p>
  <p><code>paused</code>, <code>pause()</code> and <code>resume()</code> control ambient playback. Resume continues without catching up time spent paused or hidden. A manual clock is always paused; <code>resume()</code> does not advance it. Give direct writes, a timeline or another animation driver sole ownership of these controls.</p>

  <h2 id="refresh-retry-and-dispose">Refresh, retry and dispose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>refresh(): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code> requests fresh captured paint and hides stale output. It resolves when this or a newer revision commits; it rejects on failure, invalidation, disposal or an unavailable handle.</li>
    <li><code>retry(): Promise&lt;void&gt;</code> tears down and reinitializes with retained controls. Concurrent retries share one attempt. Inspect lifecycle after it settles: the condition can still make output unavailable.</li>
    <li><code>dispose()</code> releases the attachment. It is idempotent and safe before readiness.</li>
    <li><code>onDispose(callback)</code> registers a callback that runs once, immediately if already disposed. It returns an unsubscribe function.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Refresh and control writes cannot recover an unavailable handle. Retry on a disposed handle rejects. Call <code>dispose()</code> on component unmount and handle rejected refresh/retry promises within that component's lifetime.</p>

  <h2 id="bind-gsap">Bind GSAP</h2>
  <p><code>bindFire(effect, timeline, options)</code> accepts any live manual <code>EffectHandle</code>, including non-Fire effects. The export retains its existing name. Required options are destination <code>time</code>, <code>intensity</code> and nonnegative <code>duration</code>. Optional <code>position</code> accepts GSAP insertion syntax; <code>disposeEffect</code> defaults to false.</p>
  <p>The binding snapshots starting controls and interpolates them linearly. Its <code>dispose()</code> removes only its tween unless effect ownership was explicitly transferred. The caller retains ownership of the timeline.</p>
  <p><code>bindFireAsync(promise, timeline, options)</code> also accepts a promise of any effect handle. It requires <code>disposeEffect: true</code> and immediately exposes <code>dispose()</code> and <code>ready</code>. It releases late results after cancellation or context revert. Use <code>context.revert()</code> for context cleanup.</p>
  <p>See the <a href="/docs/gsap">GSAP guide</a> for a complete sequence and teardown.</p>

  <h2 id="package-boundaries">Package responsibilities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>@domfx/core</code> defines shared handles and capture contracts without DOM, GPU or framework dependencies.</li>
    <li><code>@domfx/browser</code> owns DOM capture, silhouette preparation, GPU programs and decorative output. Its build includes shader transforms; consumers need no shader plugin or private renderer imports.</li>
    <li><code>@domfx/gsap</code> optionally binds shared controls to a caller-owned GSAP timeline. Standalone attachments do not load GSAP.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For local debugging, the experimental entry provides <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#trace-an-attachment">bounded attachment traces</a>. Keep that debugging interface separate from application control logic.</p>
</Documentation>
